= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Suppose $a\in\operatorname{acl}(A)$ in the sense of <model-theoretic algebraic closure>. Some formula $\varphi(x,\bar c)$ over $A$ has exactly $n<\infty$ realizations in the <monster model> and includes $a$. Every model $M$ containing $A$ is an <elementary substructure> of the monster and therefore contains $n$ distinct realizations. Since there are only $n$ in the monster, it contains all of them, including $a$. Hence
$$
\boxed{a\in\operatorname{acl}(A)\Longrightarrow a\in M\text{ for every model }M\supseteq A.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Suppose $a\notin\operatorname{acl}(A)$. Choose a small model $M_0$ containing $A$. The <complete type> $\operatorname{tp}(a/A)$ is nonalgebraic, so choose a realization $a'\notin M_0$. Strong homogeneity of the <monster model> gives an automorphism $\sigma$ fixing $A$ with $\sigma(a)=a'$. Then $M=\sigma^{-1}(M_0)$ contains $A$, but $a\in M$ would imply $a'=\sigma(a)\in M_0$. Taking the contrapositive proves
$$
\boxed{a\in M\text{ for every model }M\supseteq A\Longrightarrow a\in\operatorname{acl}(A).}
$$

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $B=N\setminus\operatorname{acl}(A)$. We seek a model containing $A$ and omitting every element of $B$. Every finite set of these omission requirements is satisfiable: if a finite $C\subseteq B$ met every model containing $A$, the supplied result would imply $C\cap\operatorname{acl}(A)\ne\varnothing$, a contradiction.

Apply the <compactness theorem> to the elementary-diagram formulation of these requirements, using the <Tarski-Vaught test> to axiomatize the selected elementary submodel. It gives a model $M$ containing $A$ and omitting all of $B$. Part (a) gives $\operatorname{acl}(A)\subseteq M\cap N$, while omission of $B=N\setminus\operatorname{acl}(A)$ gives the reverse inclusion. Therefore
$$
\boxed{M\cap N=\operatorname{acl}(A).}
$$

